From b3d535247ef16e8f00467735a979551d309a2659 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hamid <badi.hamid@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 09:57:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Drop the global using namespace std from an example

thermoacoustic-elasticity-axisymmetry-2d is the only one of the fifty-seven
examples that opens both namespaces, and under a compiler defaulting to C++20
it stops compiling: sl::integral collides with the std::integral concept, and
every call to integral() in it is ambiguous. gcc 16 defaults to C++20, so this
is not hypothetical. It is the same collision that the public headers were
cleaned of; this is the last place it survived.

The directive was almost unused already -- std::string, std::to_string,
std::vector, std::pow, std::cout and std::endl are qualified throughout. What
was left are the bare pow and sqrt calls, and those need care rather than a
blanket prefix: T0 is a double, while Cp, c and gamma are sparselizard
expressions. So pow(T0,n) and sqrt on doubles become std::, while
sqrt(Cp*(gamma-1)/T0) and pow(c,2) stay unqualified and keep resolving to sl::.
Getting that backwards would compile and mean something else.

Add <cmath> for the std:: maths now named explicitly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../main.cpp                                      | 15 +++++++--------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/examples/thermoacoustic-elasticity-axisymmetry-2d/main.cpp b/examples/thermoacoustic-elasticity-axisymmetry-2d/main.cpp
index 92ba313d..4dd3e425 100644
--- a/examples/thermoacoustic-elasticity-axisymmetry-2d/main.cpp
+++ b/examples/thermoacoustic-elasticity-axisymmetry-2d/main.cpp
@@ -11,12 +11,11 @@
 
 
 #include "sparselizard.h"
+#include <cmath>
 #include <iostream>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <string>
 
-using namespace std;
-
 using namespace sl;
 
 // Arguments are:
@@ -74,12 +73,12 @@ int main(void)
